  "body": " Day 6: The Sandwich Theorem (cont.) and Introduction to Continuity (Section 2.5a)    Last Time: We learned how to connect asymptotes with limits and how to evaluate limits at infinity. We also addressed a few loose ends and strange situations using the Sandwich Theorem.   Today: We'll wrap up a fact takeaway from the Sandwich Theorem before transitioning from evaluating limits to exploring the powerful things they enable. Then, we'll begin discussing what it means for a function to be continuous.     The Sandwich (or Squeeze) Theorem  We recall the Sandwich Theorem (or Squeeze Theorem):     Diagram illustrating the Sandwich Theorem with f(x) trapped between upper function h(x) and lower function g(x), all meeting at L.    Suppose   on some interval containing (except possibly at itself). If       ,  then we must have  also.   Note: In the theorem, can be finite or infinite.      Checkpoint (Exercise)    Graph showing a function u(x) bounded between two functions that meet at x = 0.    Given the figure on the left, find .      A Consequence of the Sandwich Theorem  Applying the Sandwich Theorem, we have the following useful fact :     .   Unit circle geometric diagram illustrating the inequality used to prove the limit of sin(theta)\/theta as theta approaches 0.      Problem 1  Find .  That's the end of evaluating limits we have all the building blocks we need to evaluate lots of things at this point.  We can start exploring how limits enable us to do many powerful things in some ways, limits are the machinery that actually makes calculus work.      Continuity  Before moving on from limits, let's revisit the relationship between one- and two-sided limits and what limits can—and cannot—tell us about functions.  We recall that the two-sided limit  if and only if (exactly when):    The right-hand limit     AND The left-hand limit     AND      We also recall that the existence of can tell us what is, or whether this is even a point that exists. Let's look at some possibilities.    Problem 2  Match each of the following conditions to a graph that satisfies it:   and both exist and are equal  and both exist but are not equal  exists but is undefined  does not exist but is defined      Figure 1: Graph of a function at x = a illustrating one of the four limit and function value cases.   Figure 1:    Figure 3: Graph of a function at x = a illustrating one of the four limit and function value cases.   Figure 3:      Figure 2: Graph of a function at x = a illustrating one of the four limit and function value cases.   Figure 2:    Figure 4: Graph of a function at x = a illustrating one of the four limit and function value cases.   Figure 4:     Of the functions above, which do you like best? Why? Which one is it easiest to find limits for?  You probably said the function what we've discovered here is the idea of .      Definition  A function is continuous at the point if and only if (exactly when):    The limit exists    AND The value is defined    AND   .     Notes:     To prove that is continuous at , we need to check that conditions above are met.    To show is not continuous at , we need to check that of the conditions is not met.    If is continuous at every point in its domain, we say simply that is .    Intuitively, a continuous function is one you can sketch lifting your pencil off the paper.    Let's work together to practice using this definition:    Problem 3  Show that is continuous at . Is this function continuous everywhere (i.e., at every point on the number line), or is there any point at which the function is not continuous?  For many functions (like this one), there may be some points where the function is continuous and others where it's not. There are some families of functions that are really nice and are continuous everywhere!      Continuous Functions  The following functions are continuous at every point in their :   Polynomials  Exponential Functions  Logarithmic Functions  Trig Functions (the 6 basic ones the domain is an important qualifier here!)  Sums, differences, products, and compositions of continuous functions.  Quotients of continuous functions wherever the denominator is not .   We care about continuity because if we know a function is , finding a limit is as simple as using just plug in the point!    Problem 4  Let be a constant. Find the value of that makes the following function continuous on .  Hint: It suffices to find the value of that makes continuous at .   Next time we'll think about what it means for a function to be discontinuous , and whether there's anything we can learn from that. We'll also talk about another theorem that continuity helps us with.    "
