  "body": " Day 7: Section 2.5b: Discontinuities and the Intermediate Value Theorem    Last Time: We learned a fact takeaway from the Sandwich Theorem, and the idea of continuity continuous functions are really nice, especially for finding limits!   Today: Continuing our discussion of continuity, we want to explore the ways in which a function can fail to be continuous and how to detect them. We'll also talk about another theorem that continuity helps us with.     Continuity Revisit  A function is continuous at the point if and only if (exactly when):    exists    AND is defined    AND      There are 4 ways that a function can be discontinuous , 3 that are common and one that's really rare.    1 - Removable Discontinuity  A removable discontinuity is one that can be algebraically.  Graphically, it looks like a  in an otherwise nice function (see the left figure). The missing point may or may not be defined elsewhere. Now, draw a second figure on the right by modifying the figure on the left, while keeping the removable discontinuity.    Graph of a continuous curve with a single open hole showing a removable discontinuity.    Blank coordinate grid for sketching a modified graph with a removable discontinuity.      Problem 1  We observe that the function is not continuous at because is . So there is a here, but notice that still . In fact,  . So, is a discontinuity.    How do we remove a removable discontinuity?  Since the limit exists, you can remove the removable discontinuity entirely just by filling in that single missing point with a definition:   Let's see what this function looks like on a graph: Desmos Example .   Recap  So removable discontinuities can be recognized geometrically as places where we can fill in a single point to the discontinuity. That is, these are places where  but is or is to the limit.       2 - Infinite Discontinuity  Looking at our last problem Desmos Example , do you see another spot that looks like a discontinuity?  As we lift our pencil, vertical asymptotes also represent !  Vertical asymptotes occur at values of where at least one one-sided limit is . We call them discontinuities .  In order to claim we have a vertical asymptote, we must show that the limit is from either the right-hand or left-hand side!  In the previous problem, has an infinite discontinuity at  due to a vertical asymptote at  . Indeed,   Vertical asymptotes result in discontinuities because infinite limits . What's another way a limit could fail to exist?    3 - Jump Discontinuity  A jump discontinuity occurs at a value where fails to exist because both one-sided limits, and , but are , i.e.,   , where and are real numbers (see the left figure). This can happen whether or not is defined, or what it is.  Now, draw a second and third figure on the right by modifying the figure on the left, while keeping the discontinuity.    Left graph illustrating a jump discontinuity with different left-hand and right-hand limits at x = a.    Middle graph template for modifying the jump discontinuity at x = a.    Right graph template for modifying the jump discontinuity at x = a.    Jump discontinuities happen most often in functions.    Problem 2   Find all jump discontinuities in the function  How many discontinuities do you think we have in this function? Let's sanity check with a graph: Desmos . Based on our graph, it looks like we have discontinuity. Let's use limits to prove this is true:      Problem 2 (cont.)   Find all jump discontinuities in the function  How many discontinuities do you think we have in this function? Let's sanity check with a graph: Desmos . Based on our graph, it looks like we have discontinuity. Let's use limits to prove this is true:    Recap  So to find a jump discontinuity, we show that we have both one-sided limits that but . We look for these discontinuities at the -values where piecewise functions change the way they're defined.    4 - Wild Oscillation Discontinuity  The last type of discontinuity is rare, and you probably won't come across another example. Let's look at the graph of .    Graph of the Topologist's Sine Curve f(x) = sin(1\/x) oscillating infinitely rapidly between -1 and 1 as x approaches 0.      This function is called the Topologist's Sine Curve .    What is ?    This limit since is simultaneously getting infinitely close to every -value between and . We call such behavior a  discontinuity .    We expect you to find and identify many of these, but we do want you to know they exist.        Discontinuity Recap   That concludes our study of discontinuities.   When a problem asks to find and classify discontinuities, you must identify the point(s) of discontinuity with justification and state their type using limits .        Introduction to the Intermediate Value Theorem  Our last topic for the day is the Intermediate Value Theorem . This is a theorem that tells us something really powerful about the way continuous functions behave. Answer the following:    Can you draw a function that   Goes through the points and .  Never crosses the -axis?     Can you draw a continuous function on that does all of these things?      The Intermediate Value Theorem (IVT)  If is a function on a interval , then attains value between and somewhere on the interval .    In other words, you can not move from one point to another in the direction without also going through every -value between your  -value and your  -value.    It is exactly like physical movement. If you start a drive at an elevation of feet (i.e., ), dip down into a lower valley, climb over a -foot peak, and finally stop at a -foot pass (i.e., ), you can not magically skip the -foot mark (i.e., ). You had to pass through it at some exact moment in time (i.e., ).      Graph illustrating the Intermediate Value Theorem where a continuous curve from (a, f(a)) to (b, f(b)) intersects the horizontal line y = y_0 at least once at x = c.      Algebraically, by applying the Intermediate Value Theorem, the equation  has one solution on  if    .    That is, the IVT is helpful because it can prove that a function has to hit a certain  value forcing us to actually find the point where that happens.        Problem 4  Show that has a root on the interval .    Recall: A root of a function is a number that makes the value of equal to . In other words, it is a solution to the equation .      Later in the semester, we will learn a tool to show that has exactly one root.        "
